## Actual native SSE recovery and resumed delivery: PASS

The owned Claude launcher was tested against both source and installed
f87747a with a local text-only model fixture. A synthetic 400-second-old
private queue entry authorized MCP auto recovery; a second call hit the
900-second cooldown. The production launcher claimed the durable restart,
replaced its native child, renewed socket/MCP, resumed with --continue, and
delivered the original entry exactly once (queued -> submitted -> replied).
MCP calls left queue/history/cursor bytes and mtimes unchanged; the normal
subsequent delivery appended lifecycle observations without deleting prefixes.
The owned router listener was absent after teardown. See
`evidence/sse-native/sanitized-source-installed-results.json` and the harness.
The old age was deliberately seeded and the owned launcher briefly suspended
for fixture setup; this does not prove a real external transport outage.

# Not verified
- No production runtime, real provider credential, remote gateway host or
  existing session state was used. The isolated native CLI test above covers
  actual local router dispatch; it does not establish remote-host behavior.
- No pixel screenshot; no real menu-triggered restart.
- The original affected session's route (local router versus direct gateway)
  is still unidentified. A 503 does not prove the upstream executed nothing;
  the runtime bounds only its own replay and does not claim server-side
  exactly-once.
- Direct native coverage is bounded by the measured contracts above.

